%% A fresh story for the VoxDown examples, free to copy (CC0). %%

# The Dragon Who Would Not Roar

{book only} For everyone who has ever been woken up too early.

## The proclamation {hidden}

@(warmly) Sir Percival had been training his entire life for this day.

He had polished his armor until it squeaked. [armor squeaking] He had practiced his battle cry in front of the mirror. He had even read a book about dragons, although he had skipped the chapter called *Things Dragons Find Annoying*.

@King (grandly, to a crowded hall) “Whoever brings back the dragon of Mount Mumble,” proclaimed the King, “shall have half my kingdom and all of my biscuits.”

@Percival “All of them?” said Sir Percival.

@King (less grandly) “Most of them.”

{page}

## The mountain {hidden}

Mount Mumble was very tall, and very quiet. [wind across rocks] Sir Percival climbed it all morning. Clank, clank, clank.

Then he heard a deep rumble. [deep rumbling, from inside a cave]

The dragon was snoring. @Gerald [snores]

::: whispering
Sir Percival crept inside.
Clank.
Clank.
{pause}
The dragon opened one eye.
:::

@Gerald (deep, polite and utterly unbothered) “Could you not?”

Sir Percival stopped.

@Gerald “I’ve only just got to sleep,” said the dragon. “I was up all night counting sheep.”

@Percival “Real sheep?”

@Gerald “Imaginary ones. They were very difficult to catch.”

{page}

## The battle {hidden}

%% Percival talks himself up here. Let him get louder line by line. %%

::: @Percival trying to sound brave
I am Sir Percival of the Shining Shield!
I have come to defeat you!
Prepare to meet thy doom!
:::

@Gerald [yawns] “Is it a long doom? Only I’ve got a nap at four.”

@Percival “Aren’t you going to roar?”

The dragon sighed.

@Gerald (with no enthusiasm whatsoever) “Rooooar.”

@Percival “That was the worst roar I have ever heard.”

@Gerald “Thank you,” said the dragon. @(proudly) “I’ve been practicing.”

{pause 2s}

{page}

## Home {hidden}

So Sir Percival went home without a dragon, but with a jar of the dragon’s very best soup, which is better than half a kingdom on a cold night. [door closes; a fire crackling]

@King (suspicious) “Where is the dragon?”

@Percival “Asleep,” said Sir Percival. “I thought it best not to wake him.”

@(fondly) And to this day, if you climb Mount Mumble very quietly, you can hear the dragon snoring. @Gerald [snores, contentedly]

{audio only} Thank you for listening.

{book only} The End
